# AAROHAN AI — IMAGE GENERATION ENGINE 1.0
## COMPLETE IMAGE STUDIO IMPLEMENTATION SPECIFICATION

PROJECT: AAROHAN AI
OWNER: Datta Sable
REPOSITORY: https://github.com/sabledattatray/aarohan-ai

OBJECTIVE:

Implement a fully functional AI image-generation and image-editing system inside AAROHAN.

Users should be able to describe an image in ordinary language, and AAROHAN should understand the request, prepare an optimized image prompt, select an available generation model, generate an image and return the actual image inside the application.

This must work as a real product.

Do not create a mock image generator.

Do not return stock images as generated images.

Do not display fake progress or simulated generation results.

==================================================
PHASE 1 — REPOSITORY INSPECTION
==================================================

Before implementation:

1. Inspect the complete existing repository.
2. Identify the frontend framework.
3. Identify the backend framework.
4. Inspect the current Chat API.
5. Inspect the existing Dataset Studio.
6. Inspect existing file storage.
7. Inspect current model-management services.
8. Inspect GPU detection and configuration.
9. Identify the current image-generation dependencies.
10. Preserve all working features.

Create:

docs/IMAGE_ENGINE_ARCHITECTURE.md
docs/IMAGE_ENGINE_SETUP.md
docs/IMAGE_ENGINE_STATUS.md

==================================================
PHASE 2 — IMAGE STUDIO UI
==================================================

Create a complete Image Studio.

Route:

/app/images

DESIGN:

Follow the existing AAROHAN dark interface.

Use a premium creative workspace layout.

MAIN COMPONENTS:

1. Prompt composer.
2. Image preview area.
3. Generation settings.
4. Model selector.
5. Style selector.
6. Aspect ratio selector.
7. Generation history.
8. Image library.
9. Download controls.
10. Editing tools.

PROMPT INPUT:

Allow natural-language prompts.

Examples:

"Create a realistic futuristic city at sunset."

"Create a premium technology company logo."

"Design a professional LinkedIn banner."

"Generate a cinematic photograph of a mountain landscape."

"Create a 3D illustration of a neural network."

"Create an educational illustration of the human digestive system."

The interface must support multiline prompts.

Add an Enhance Prompt button.

Add Generate Image button.

Add Stop Generation button.

Add Regenerate button.

Add Clear button.

==================================================
PHASE 3 — IMAGE GENERATION BACKEND
==================================================

Implement a modular image-generation provider architecture.

Primary local provider:

COMFYUI

Repository:
https://github.com/Comfy-Org/ComfyUI

Integrate ComfyUI through its documented API.

Features:

- Backend health check.
- Connection status.
- Workflow submission.
- Queue monitoring.
- Generation progress.
- Job cancellation where supported.
- Result retrieval.
- Error handling.
- Image metadata retrieval.

Default local endpoint:

http://127.0.0.1:8188

Make the endpoint configurable.

Do not hardcode it throughout the application.

Create a provider interface:

ImageGenerationProvider

Required methods:

- health_check()
- list_models()
- generate()
- edit()
- get_job_status()
- cancel_job()
- retrieve_result()

Create separate adapters for:

- ComfyUI.
- Compatible local Diffusers pipelines.
- Optional external image-generation APIs.

The application must continue functioning when an optional provider is unavailable.

==================================================
PHASE 4 — MODEL MANAGEMENT
==================================================

Implement a model registry.

Support configurable models.

INITIAL MODEL STRATEGY:

1. Stable Diffusion 1.5: initial local baseline.
2. SDXL Base: optional higher-quality model.
3. Compatible image-to-image models.
4. Compatible inpainting models.
5. Compatible upscaling models.

Do not automatically download large models.

Show:

- Model name.
- Model type.
- File size.
- Model path.
- Download status.
- Installation status.
- Hardware compatibility.
- Model license.
- Active status.

Before downloading a model:

- Show its size.
- Show required disk space.
- Show license information.
- Ask for confirmation.

Never download models from unknown or untrusted sources.

==================================================
PHASE 5 — HARDWARE OPTIMIZATION
==================================================

Target hardware:

NVIDIA RTX 2060 6GB VRAM.

Design for limited VRAM.

Implement:

- FP16 where supported.
- Low-VRAM mode.
- CPU offloading.
- Sequential model loading.
- Memory monitoring.
- Model unloading.
- Configurable batch size.
- Generation queue.
- Automatic memory cleanup.
- Safe fallback when CUDA is unavailable.

INITIAL GENERATION DEFAULTS:

- One image per request.
- 512x512 initial resolution for lightweight models.
- Configurable resolution.
- Conservative inference steps.
- No automatic simultaneous loading of large models.

Allow higher resolutions only when the selected backend supports them within available memory.

Do not crash the main AAROHAN application if image generation runs out of memory.

Return a useful error message and recovery instructions.

==================================================
PHASE 6 — IMAGE CREATION CAPABILITIES
==================================================

Support the following generation categories:

PHOTOGRAPHY
- Portraits.
- Landscapes.
- Architecture.
- Product photography.
- Food photography.
- Nature.
- Travel.
- Wildlife.

DIGITAL ART
- Digital painting.
- Fantasy.
- Sci-fi.
- Anime.
- Cartoon.
- Illustration.
- Concept art.
- 3D renders.

BUSINESS AND BRANDING
- Logos.
- Brand illustrations.
- Social media graphics.
- LinkedIn banners.
- Blog featured images.
- Marketing posters.
- Product advertisements.
- Presentation backgrounds.

EDUCATION
- Scientific illustrations.
- Technical diagrams.
- Educational graphics.
- Historical scene illustrations.
- Infographics.

DESIGN
- Website hero concepts.
- UI/UX concepts.
- App illustrations.
- Interior concepts.
- Architecture concepts.

Do not assume every model performs equally well in every category.

==================================================
PHASE 7 — ADVANCED IMAGE CONTROLS
==================================================

Provide:

- Width.
- Height.
- Aspect ratio.
- Number of images.
- Seed.
- Guidance scale.
- Inference steps.
- Negative prompt.
- Sampler where supported.
- Scheduler where supported.

ASPECT RATIOS:

1:1
4:3
3:4
16:9
9:16
3:2
2:3
21:9

Add preset buttons:

- Square.
- Landscape.
- Portrait.
- Story.
- Banner.
- Wide cinematic.

Validate dimensions against the selected model and workflow.

==================================================
PHASE 8 — PROMPT INTELLIGENCE
==================================================

Integrate the existing AAROHAN language model and conversation engine.

Users should not need to understand technical image prompts.

Example:

User:

"Make a nice image of a futuristic office."

AAROHAN should understand the request and generate a detailed image prompt.

Prompt enhancement should consider:

- Subject.
- Environment.
- Composition.
- Lighting.
- Camera perspective.
- Color palette.
- Materials.
- Visual style.
- Detail level.
- Aspect ratio.

Preserve the user's original intent.

Allow the user to view and edit the enhanced prompt.

Implement spelling correction.

Understand informal English and common Hinglish instructions.

Do not automatically change a user's requested visual style.

==================================================
PHASE 9 — IMAGE EDITING
==================================================

Implement actual image-editing workflows where supported by the selected model.

Features:

1. Image-to-image.
2. Inpainting.
3. Outpainting.
4. Background removal.
5. Background replacement.
6. Image upscaling.
7. Image variation.
8. Style transformation.
9. Object replacement.
10. Cropping and resizing.

Allow image uploads.

Show an original-versus-edited preview.

Preserve the original file.

Do not claim a specific editing capability works until its model and workflow are installed and tested.

==================================================
PHASE 10 — IMAGE LIBRARY
==================================================

Create a persistent image library.

Features:

- Generated images.
- Uploaded reference images.
- Search.
- Filters.
- Tags.
- Favorites.
- Collections.
- Rename.
- Delete.
- Download.
- Image details.
- Prompt history.

Save:

- Image ID.
- Prompt.
- Enhanced prompt.
- Model name.
- Seed.
- Width.
- Height.
- Generation date.
- File path.
- Provider.
- Generation settings.

Store image metadata in the existing database.

Store image files in managed storage.

Avoid storing large image binaries directly in relational database records.

==================================================
PHASE 11 — IMAGE GENERATION HISTORY
==================================================

Display:

- Queued.
- Processing.
- Completed.
- Failed.
- Cancelled.

For every generation job, retain:

- Job ID.
- User prompt.
- Model.
- Start time.
- Completion time.
- Status.
- Error message.
- Generated files.

Allow users to regenerate using previous settings.

==================================================
PHASE 12 — CHAT INTEGRATION
==================================================

Integrate image generation directly into AI Chat.

Example:

User:

"Create an image of a futuristic AI laboratory."

AAROHAN:

Understands the request.
Selects image-generation tool.
Creates an image.
Displays the generated result.

Users should not need to open Image Studio for every generation request.

Support conversational editing:

"Make it brighter."

"Change the background to blue."

"Make it more realistic."

"Generate another variation."

"Create a landscape version."

Maintain image-generation context across follow-up messages.

Use the actual previous image as the editing input when required.

==================================================
PHASE 13 — IMAGE SAFETY AND SECURITY
==================================================

Implement:

- Prompt validation.
- Upload validation.
- File-size limits.
- File-type validation.
- Safe storage.
- Access control.
- Generation permissions.
- Content moderation.
- Rate limiting.
- Resource limits.

Respect model licenses and usage restrictions.

Block prohibited image requests.

Protect against non-consensual intimate imagery, exploitation, deceptive impersonation and other harmful uses.

Do not expose private uploaded images.

==================================================
PHASE 14 — API ENDPOINTS
==================================================

Implement endpoints consistent with the existing API conventions.

Required capabilities:

POST /api/images/generate
POST /api/images/edit
POST /api/images/enhance-prompt
GET /api/images/jobs
GET /api/images/jobs/{job_id}
POST /api/images/jobs/{job_id}/cancel
GET /api/images/library
GET /api/images/library/{image_id}
DELETE /api/images/library/{image_id}
GET /api/images/providers
GET /api/images/models
GET /api/images/health

Use proper request validation and response schemas.

Do not duplicate existing routes if equivalent functionality exists.

